    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe `getRiskFlag()` getter itself is well-placed and idiomatic \u2014 it fits the existing family of derived `@Transient` getters (`getTimezone`, `getOwnerSegment`, `getContactPreference`, `getIdentityKey`), and delegating the email check to a pure static helper mirrors the existing `soundex` precedent. The issue is the disposable-provider data the helper relies on.\n\n**Finding 1 \u2014 the disposable-provider list is duplicated across two layers (real maintenance hazard).**\nThe same real-world fact \u2014 the set of known disposable providers \u2014 now lives in two places, in two representations:\n- `OwnerEmails.DISPOSABLE_DOMAINS` = `{mailinator.com, tempmail.com, guerrillamail.com}` (full domains, exact-match hard rejection), in `rest/function/owner/OwnerEmails.java:16`.\n- `Owner.DISPOSABLE_BASE_LABELS` = `{mailinator, tempmail, guerrillamail}` (base labels, adjacency), in `model/Owner.java:623`.\n\nAdding a new provider (say `sharklasers.com`) requires editing both lists, in different formats, in different packages; forgetting the model copy silently drops the risk signal while the hard rejection still works. That the constant carries a ~8-line javadoc arguing it is \"not a duplicated list\" is itself a signal that it is one. The two *matching rules* (exact vs. adjacent) are legitimately distinct concerns, but the *provider identity set* underneath them is not \u2014 that should be a single source with two views.\n\nThe javadoc's own justification points at the fix: it says `riskFlag` \"must not depend on the request-function layer that owns the hard-rejection blocklist.\" Correct \u2014 but the resolution is to invert the ownership, not to copy the data. The model is the lower, more fundamental layer; the canonical provider set belongs there (on `Owner`, or a shared constants holder in the model/util package), and `OwnerEmails` should derive its full-domain check from that single source (`rest \u2192 model` is the natural dependency direction). Right now the fact is owned in the upper layer and re-copied downward, which is the awkward/inverted arrangement.\n\n**Finding 2 \u2014 domain-extraction logic is re-implemented.**\n`isDisposableAdjacentDomain` re-does the `lastIndexOf('@')` + `toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT)` extraction that `OwnerEmails.domainOf` (`OwnerEmails.java:27`) already performs. Once the shared source lives where both can see it, the extraction helper should be shared too rather than written twice.\n\n**Minor nit.**\n`getRiskFlag()` reads `this.possibleDuplicate` / `this.capacityWarning` directly while its own javadoc links to `getPossibleDuplicate()` / `getCapacityWarning()`. The getters return the raw fields so behaviour is identical, but going through the getters (as the sibling derived getters like `getTimezone`/`getOwnerSegment` do with `getLocality()`/`getMembershipLevel()`) would be more consistent with the class's style.",
